parts.text:
The user wants a login page before index with Google sign-in, and sync Google Photos in the index, allowing photos to be placed on all menu pages.

This is a significant request. Important constraints: This is a static HTML/CSS site with no backend. Real Google Photos API requires OAuth 2.0 with a client ID, server-side token exchange, API scopes, and Google Photos Library API. It cannot be fully done client-side securely. Also, Google Photos API now requires restricted scopes and app verification; "photoslibrary.readonly" etc.

I should be honest: I can build the front-end login page (Google Sign-In button via Google Identity Services), gate index behind it, and add photo placeholders that can display photos. But actual Google Photos sync requires:
1. Google Cloud project with OAuth client ID
2. API key
3. Authorized JavaScript origins
4. Scopes
5. Ideally a backend

Also the current "index" should be protected. A purely client-side gate is not secure (anyone can bypass), but for a personal family site it's acceptable. I should implement a client-side demo: `login.html` with Google Identity Services, store a flag in localStorage/sessionStorage, redirect to index. Add a guard script in all pages that redirects to login if not signed in. Add Google Photos integration module that fetches photos via the Google Photos Library API and populates photo placeholders across pages.

But wait: Google Photos Library API `mediaItems.search` requires OAuth and returns base URLs. Client-side fetch to `https://photoslibrary.googleapis.com/v1/mediaItems:search` with an access token works (CORS? Google APIs generally allow CORS). Access tokens expire in ~1 hour, and GIS gives access token client-side. This could work without a backend for short sessions. However, to keep the session, you'd need refresh tokens (backend). So a full sync isn't robust client-side.

Given the scope and the missing client ID, I should:
1. Ask the user for their Google OAuth Client ID? Or build with a placeholder and instructions.

The instruction says avoid asking too many questions; be proactive. But a client ID is required. I can implement with a clearly marked placeholder constant `GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID = "YOUR_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID"` and instructions, plus the whole flow.

Also "permita colocar fotos en todas las paginas de los item del menu" = allow placing photos on all pages of the menu items. So create a shared photo gallery component that pulls from Google Photos (or allows adding photos) on each page. Since each page has `.photo-placeholder` elements, we can replace them with images from Google Photos or from a local album.

Design plan:
- `login.html`: Google sign-in page (GIS), matching style. On success, store user profile and access token in sessionStorage/localStorage, redirect to `index.html`.
- `auth.js`: shared auth guard + Google Photos helper. Exposes:
  - `requireAuth()`: if not logged in, redirect to login.html.
  - `initAuth()`: renders user info / logout in header maybe.
  - `getAccessToken()`.
  - `loadGooglePhotos(container, options)`: search media items and render into `.photo-placeholder` or a gallery.
- Add `<script src="auth.js" defer></script>` to all pages, and add a logout/user chip.
- On index, add a "Google Photos" section syncing photos.
- For every page, replace `.photo-placeholder` contents with synced photos when logged in; fallback to placeholder text if not.

Caveat: Google Photos API scopes. To read photos: `https://www.googleapis.com/auth/photoslibrary.readonly` (restricted). GIS token client supports it. The API call: `POST https://photoslibrary.googleapis.com/v1/mediaItems:search` with `{pageSize: 25, filters: {includeArchivedMedia: false}}` and headers Authorization Bearer. Response mediaItems[].baseUrl; append `=w1200-h800` for sizing. Need `?alt=json`.

Important: Google Photos Library API's `mediaItems:search` requires the user to have granted the scope; also as of 2025 there may be changes. Fine.

Security note: exposing client ID is normal (public). No client secret in frontend. Access token in sessionStorage. Client-side gate is not real security; mention it.

Should I use Google Identity Services (`accounts.google.com/gsi/client`) with `google.accounts.oauth2.initTokenClient`. Yes.

Implementation details:

login.html:
- Full page centered card.
- Title, subtitle.
