parts.text:
Now, there's an important issue: `auth.js` runs the guard at top level: `if (!isLoginPage() && !isLoggedIn()) location.replace(LOGIN_PAGE);`. But `login.html`'s inline script also checks. Fine.

Potential issue: `index.html` loads auth.js with defer, so guard runs after HTML parse. Brief flash possible but okay. Could move auth.js to head with defer? It's at end; guard runs on execution. Fine.

Another issue: `ensurePhotoSection()` on pages without placeholders appends a section with grid, then `loadPhotos` fetches. On gallery.html, `#google-photos-grid` doesn't exist, placeholders exist → no injected grid, fill placeholders. Good.

On index, grid exists → fill grid; no placeholders. Good.

On canada/colombia/trips, placeholders exist, no grid → fill placeholders. Good.

On family/simon-family/story, no placeholders, no grid → inject grid section. Good.

Now a concern: Google Photos `mediaItems:search` with `{pageSize:50}` and no filters — is it allowed? According to Google Photos Library API docs: "mediaItems:search" request body can be empty; if no filters are set, all media items are returned. Yes, pageSize optional, default 25. Should work.

Another concern: Google Photos API `photoslibrary.readonly` is a restricted scope. GIS token client supports requesting it. The consent screen needs it added. Fine.

CORS: `photoslibrary.googleapis.com` supports CORS? Google APIs generally do. Should be okay.

Now, the login page: `auth.js` defer loads, and inline script defer. Order: both deferred, executed in document order: auth.js first, then inline. DOMContentLoaded listeners fire after both executed. `window.SimonAuth` set synchronously at end of auth.js, so inline can access it at DOMContentLoaded. Good.

But note: auth.js top-level guard: `isLoginPage()` returns true for login.html. Good.

One more: `signOut` revokes token only if GIS loaded. On content pages GIS may not be loaded (only loaded during signIn on login page, and a new page load loses `window.google`). So revoke won't happen; we just clear session. Acceptable, though token remains valid until expiry. Could load GIS then revoke; but revoke may need the token. Let's improve: load GIS asynchronously then revoke. But signOut then immediately redirects; could clear then revoke in background. Simpler: clear and redirect; token expires in ~1h. Fine for now. Actually security best practice: revoke. Let's modify signOut to attempt loadGis then revoke before redirect, with a fallback timeout. But redirect after revoke callback. Implement:

```js
async function signOut() {
  const session = readSession();
  clearSession();
  if (session) {
    try {
      await loadGis();
      if (window.google?.accounts?.oauth2) {
        google.accounts.oauth2.revoke(session.token, () => location.replace(LOGIN_PAGE));
        return;
      }
    } catch (error) {}
  }
  location.replace(LOGIN_PAGE);
}
```
But if revoke callback never fires, user stuck. Add setTimeout fallback. Hmm. Keep simple: fire revoke without awaiting, redirect. But revoke requires google loaded. We can call loadGis().then(revoke) and redirect after 600ms max. Let's not overcomplicate; clearing session is enough for this app. I'll leave as is but ensure it doesn't break. Current signOut clears and redirects immediately. Fine.

Now, a real problem: `auth.js` references `HOME_PAGE` but unused except export. Fine.

Another: `isConfigured` check regex `/\.apps\.googleusercontent\.com$/` — the placeholder ends with `.apps.googleusercontent.com`, and startsWith("PON_") → false. Good.

Now, an important UX issue: if the user is not logged in and visits index, redirect to login.html. But the login page's `auth.js` `init()` calls `mountUserChip` (returns), `wireSyncButton` (none), `loadPhotos` (returns). Good.

Now test with a local server and maybe fetch pages to ensure no 404 for auth.js. Also maybe run a quick DOM simulation with jsdom? Not installed. Skip.

Potential CSS issue: `.login-page main` — specificity: `main` rule sets width/margin/transform; `.login-page main` (0,1,1) beats `main` (0,0,1). Good. But `header, main, footer { margin-left: 250px; }` specificity (0,0,1) vs `.login-page main` (0,1,1) → override. Good.

Also body.login-page uses `display: grid; place-items: center;` — main centered. Good. min-height 100vh.

Now update todos and maybe test by starting a server and using curl to confirm pages load. Also check that index includes the section and that guard doesn't break non-JS. Let's run a quick server test with curl for status codes.

Also I should provide the user with setup instructions: create OAuth Client ID, add authorized origin http://localhost:8000, enable Google Photos Library API, add scope, test users. Since user asked to "ejecutar" essentially build; final message should explain how to configure and run, and the limitation that this client-side gate isn't real security and Google Photos needs a backend for persistent refresh tokens.

Let me run a server briefly and curl.

